Found in the k337 security review. Content/Image.pm:_is_private_address (lines ~667-683) unwraps only ::ffff:0:0/96 (mapped) and ::/96. Reproduced with an injected resolver: 64:ff9b::a9fe:a9fe (NAT64 -> 169.254.169.254), 64:ff9b::7f00:1, 64:ff9b:1::a00:1 (RFC 8215 local NAT64), 2002:a9fe:a9fe:: and 2002:7f00:1:: (6to4), ::ffff:0:7f00:1 (SIIT translated) are all ALLOWED. On IPv6-only hosts with DNS64/NAT64 (e.g. AWS IPv6-only subnets) a name with a synthesized AAAA reaches internal IPv4 targets through the gateway. Also not listed: 198.18.0.0/15, 192.0.0.0/24 (192.0.0.170/171 NAT64 discovery). Fix: extract and re-check the embedded IPv4 for 64:ff9b::/96, 64:ff9b:1::/48 (RFC 6052 positions), 2002::/16 (bytes 2-5), ::ffff:0:0:0/96; refuse (or document) Teredo 2001::/32; extend the POD list and the address table in t/47_image_fetch_limits.t.
